(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-mi-antianginals-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Antianginal Drugs Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker both inhibits and is a substrate of cytochrome P450 3A4?","choices":["Verapamil","Amlodipine","Nifedipine","Felodipine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Verapamil (like diltiazem) is both a substrate and an inhibitor of cytochrome P450 3A4, so it raises levels of other drugs cleared by that enzyme. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which second messenger do nitrates increase to relax vascular smooth muscle?","choices":["Cyclic guanosine monophosphate","Cyclic adenosine monophosphate","Inositol trisphosphate","Cytosolic calcium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nitric oxide from nitrates stimulates conversion of guanosine triphosphate to cyclic guanosine monophosphate, which lowers calcium and relaxes smooth muscle. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which adverse effects do nitrates and dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers share?","choices":["Bradycardia and fatigue","Constipation and cough","Headache and flushing","Nightmares and dyslipidemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nitrates and dihydropyridines both cause headache and flushing, reflecting vasodilation. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which condition is a contraindication to nitrates?","choices":["Vasospastic angina","Effort-induced angina","Prior myocardial infarction","Obstructive cardiomyopathy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nitrates are contraindicated in obstructive cardiomyopathy, as well as in aortic valve stenosis and with phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which set of parameters is monitored during beta blocker therapy?","choices":["Heart rate, glucose, lipids","Potassium, creatinine, sodium","Liver enzymes, bilirubin, albumin","Platelets, clotting time, hemoglobin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers can cause bradycardia, hyperglycemia and dyslipidemia, so heart rate, blood sugar and lipids are followed. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which combination reduces symptoms of vasospastic angina?","choices":["Calcium channel blocker and nitrate","Beta blocker and nitrate","Beta blocker and calcium channel blocker","Beta blocker and aspirin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both classes relieve vasospasm and reduce symptoms; beta blockers are avoided because they may worsen them. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Why, in the traditional advice, are opened nitroglycerin tablets replaced on a schedule?","choices":["Aged tablets become toxic","Repeated use causes tolerance","Light and moisture degrade the drug","The tablets harden and cannot dissolve"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nitroglycerin is unstable, and exposure to light or moisture breaks it down so it may not work when needed; that is the reason for the traditional 3 to 6 month replacement and for keeping the tablets in the original container. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"How is nitroglycerin taken for an acute attack?","choices":["Swallowed whole with water","Chewed and swallowed","Under the tongue","Applied to the chest"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A sublingual tablet or spray is placed under the tongue for rapid absorption. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is aspirin used for in ischemic heart disease?","choices":["Preventing acute coronary syndromes","Relieving effort-induced angina","Lowering heart rate","Lowering wall tension"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin is given to all patients with ischemic heart disease without contraindications to prevent acute coronary syndromes. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which drug class is first line for chronic stable angina when no contraindication is present?","choices":["Long-acting nitrates","Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers","Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers","Beta blockers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers are first-line therapy absent a contraindication; they lower heart rate and contractility and so lower oxygen demand. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Besides relieving an acute attack, what is short-acting nitroglycerin used for?","choices":["Preventing effort-induced angina","Preventing acute coronary syndromes","Lowering mortality after infarction","Replacing daily beta blockers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Short-acting nitrates are taken before activity to prevent effort-induced angina as well as to relieve acute symptoms. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which adverse effect is typical of dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers?","choices":["Bradycardia","Nightmares","Peripheral edema","Hyperglycemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Headache, flushing and peripheral edema are the characteristic dihydropyridine effects, reflecting strong vasodilation. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which nitrate formulation is used to relieve an acute attack of angina?","choices":["Isosorbide mononitrate","Sublingual nitroglycerin","Isosorbide dinitrate","Nitroglycerin ointment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Short-acting nitroglycerin (tablet or spray under the tongue) relieves acute symptoms and can prevent effort-induced angina. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which treatment goal extends life rather than improving quality of life?","choices":["Relieving symptoms","Preventing acute coronary syndromes","Preventing symptoms","Improving exercise capacity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Preventing acute coronary syndromes increases the quantity of life; relieving and preventing symptoms improves its quality. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which condition is a contraindication to non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers?","choices":["Vasospastic angina","Acute heart failure","Severe peripheral vascular disease","Asthma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Verapamil and diltiazem depress contractility, so acute heart failure, low ejection fraction, bradycardia and heart block contraindicate them. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is the main risk of adding simvastatin to a patient who takes verapamil?","choices":["Loss of the verapamil effect","Excess slowing of the heart","Reduced absorption of simvastatin","Statin muscle and liver toxicity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Verapamil inhibits cytochrome P450 3A4, which clears simvastatin, so higher simvastatin levels raise the risk of muscle and liver toxicity. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which three factors set myocardial oxygen demand?","choices":["Arterial oxygen pressure, hemoglobin, and coronary flow","Heart rate, hemoglobin level, and coronary flow","Contractility, oxygen extraction, and coronary flow","Heart rate, contractility, and wall tension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Demand rises with a faster heart rate, stronger contraction and greater systolic wall tension; wall tension reflects ventricular volume and pressure (preload and afterload). — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which drug is a non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker?","choices":["Amlodipine","Nifedipine","Diltiazem","Felodipine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Diltiazem and verapamil are the non-dihydropyridines, which lower heart rate and slow atrioventricular conduction. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker formulation should be avoided in angina?","choices":["Long-acting amlodipine","Short-acting nifedipine","Sustained-release verapamil","Extended-release diltiazem"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Short-acting agents, nifedipine in particular, should be avoided; long-acting agents are used. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which type of angina is caused by a transient, abrupt narrowing of a coronary artery, often at night or in the early morning?","choices":["Chronic stable angina","Unstable angina","Variant (Prinzmetal) angina","Class I angina"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vasospastic angina is a transient, abrupt reduction in vessel diameter; it affects younger patients with fewer risk factors and often occurs at night. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"In the traditional comorbidity-based approach, which drug type is avoided for angina in a patient with diabetes?","choices":["Cardioselective beta blockers","Long-acting nitrates","Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers","Non-cardioselective beta blockers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Non-cardioselective beta blockers are the type avoided in diabetes, because beta blockers can raise blood sugar and mask the warning signs of hypoglycemia; a cardioselective one is the listed alternative. Current practice is less absolute: beta blockers are not contraindicated in diabetes, but a cardioselective one is preferred and the patient is taught about masked hypoglycemia. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"How is a nitroglycerin patch or ointment scheduled through the day?","choices":["Worn continuously, changed daily","Applied only when pain starts","6 hours on, then 18 hours off","12 hours on, then 12 hours off"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The patch or ointment is worn for 12 hours and left off for 12 hours each day. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What role do angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors play in stable coronary artery disease?","choices":["They relieve acute angina attacks","They lower myocardial oxygen consumption","They may slow disease progression","They prevent effort-induced angina"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They do not relieve angina, but may slow progression and are used in coronary disease with diabetes or left ventricular systolic dysfunction. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A man taking isosorbide mononitrate asks about erectile dysfunction drugs. What is the counseling?","choices":["Avoid combining them with nitrates","Safe if the nitrate is a skin patch","Safe if taken with a meal","Safe if the nitrate is short-acting"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sildenafil, tadalafil and vardenafil with any nitrate can cause hypotension, myocardial infarction or stroke. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A patient with angina has a heart rate of 50 beats per minute and atrioventricular block. Which class is first line?","choices":["Beta blockers","Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers","Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers","Long-acting nitrates"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. With bradycardia or atrioventricular block, a dihydropyridine is first line; it does not slow atrioventricular conduction. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"When left ventricular function is decreased and a beta blocker cannot be used, which calcium channel blocker is the alternative?","choices":["Verapamil","Diltiazem","Amlodipine","Nifedipine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers are first line with decreased left ventricular function; amlodipine is the calcium channel blocker alternative. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Where should nitroglycerin tablets be stored?","choices":["In a daily pill organizer","In the bathroom cabinet","In the refrigerator","In the original container"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tablets belong in the original packaging in a cool, dry place. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which class is most likely responsible for nightmares and sexual dysfunction?","choices":["Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers","Beta blockers","Long-acting nitrates","Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fatigue, sexual dysfunction, nightmares and worsened claudication are listed adverse reactions of beta blockers. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"In the traditional comorbidity-based approach to angina therapy, which drug class is best avoided after a myocardial infarction?","choices":["Beta blockers","Calcium channel blockers","Long-acting nitrates","Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The traditional comorbidity table lists calcium channel blockers as the class to avoid after an infarction, because a beta blocker is preferred on the mortality evidence. Current practice is less absolute: a non-dihydropyridine may substitute when a beta blocker cannot be used and ventricular function is normal. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which history makes a beta blocker the preferred antianginal?","choices":["Prior myocardial infarction","Sinus bradycardia","Reactive airway disease","Second-degree atrioventricular block"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A beta blocker is given after a myocardial infarction unless contraindicated, and is the first-line antianginal in that setting. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which adverse effect do beta blockers, calcium channel blockers, and nitrates share?","choices":["Bradycardia","Peripheral edema","Sexual dysfunction","Hypotension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All three antianginal classes lower systolic pressure, and hypotension is listed for each. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which antianginal class lowers oxygen demand mainly by reducing ventricular volume?","choices":["Beta blockers","Nitrates","Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers","Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nitrates lower left ventricular volume the most, which lowers wall tension; they raise heart rate reflexively rather than lowering it. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 25"}]};
